Team 3 — The Market

This one could be nasty.

Don’t deeply reason through every candidate.

Generate cheap competing hypotheses:

H_1,H_2,\ldots,H_n

Give each a tiny semantic budget.

They bid for additional computation based on:

\text{surprise}
+\text{evidence}
+\text{potential consequence}

Weak ideas die without explanation.

Promising ones receive more tokens.

So computation becomes an attention market.

\boxed{\text{Tokens become investment capital}}

Instead of spending 2,000 tokens proving one route, spend 20 tokens each scouting 30 routes and allocate the remaining budget to the best three.

That’s very different from Pantheon.

And it integrates naturally with your Idea Bond Market later—but it should first compete independently.

⸻

Team 4 — The Mycelium

Style: Distributed associative activation.

Don’t retrieve whole concepts.

Activate tiny neighboring fragments:

x\rightarrow\{n_1,n_2,n_3\}

Those activate their neighbors.

Stop when two activation fronts unexpectedly meet:

\[
A\rightsquigarrow X\leftsquigarrow B
\]

The meeting point is a candidate bridge.

This is particularly interesting for novelty search because we aren’t asking:

What connects A and B?

We’re allowing cheap local propagation from both until their neighborhoods collide.

That’s a different search algorithm.

Pantheon routes deliberately.

Mycelium grows until paths touch.

⸻

Team 5 — The Jazz Band

Pantheon likes defined operators.

Jazz Band says:

Don’t encode the operator. Encode constraints and let the operation emerge.

Give me:

\{\text{key},\text{tempo},\text{forbidden moves},\text{goal}\}

rather than a procedure.

Then improvise inside the admissible space.

For reasoning:

\boxed{\text{small boundary specification}\rightarrow\text{large emergent trajectory}}

That’s potentially incredibly token-efficient.

Instead of telling me how to think, specify:

preserve X; challenge Y; don’t assume Z; return only novel consequences.

Four constraints might generate hundreds of possible reasoning moves.

That’s constraint programming through language.

⸻

Team 6 — The Hologram

This rival attacks the assumption that every piece of information needs its own address.

Store the same important relational structure redundantly across multiple tiny fragments.

Then any sufficiently rich subset reconstructs an approximation of the whole.

\{f_1,f_4,f_9\}\rightarrow\hat M

Different subset:

\{f_2,f_5,f_7\}\rightarrow\hat M'

and:

\hat M\approx\hat M'

This trades perfect precision for robust reconstruction from partial context.

Very useful when context windows shift or retrieval is incomplete.

Pantheon has Semantic Hyperlinks.

Hologram says:

Stop depending on finding the right link. Make the important structure recoverable from many directions.

