But your prompt triggered a stranger possibility

We’ve been treating tokens as the medium in which cognition must happen.

That’s unnecessarily restrictive.

Tokens can instead become control packets for processes whose state lives elsewhere.

Think CPU architecture.

A processor doesn’t repeatedly describe its entire memory state in every instruction.

It says:

\texttt{ADD R1,R2}

because registers and memory already hold the operands.

Our equivalent:

\boxed{\text{tokens = control plane}}

while:

\boxed{\text{external structured state = data plane}}

The PatternDex, semantic graph, repo, ledgers, tests, artifacts, embeddings, code execution, files, and persistent structures carry state.

Language mostly says:

retrieve this; transform that; compare these; store delta there.

That’s a much more radical token optimization than abbreviation.

Move cognition’s persistent state out of the conversational stream.

Then my active context becomes something like CPU cache rather than the entire hard drive.

